This practical keeps the points fixed and changes one construction choice at a time. To use another point cloud, replace the circle array by an \(n\times2\) coordinate array and rerun the radius sweep:
\[S\subset\mathbb R^2 \longrightarrow \{B(x,r)\}_{x\in S}
\longrightarrow \check C_r(S)\ \text{or}\ \operatorname{Rips}_r(S)
\longrightarrow H_p(-;\mathbb F_2).\]
We use the ball-radius convention: Čech uses balls of radius \(r\), while Rips contains simplices of diameter at most \(2r\). With this convention the two complexes have the same edges and \(\check C_r(S)\subseteq\operatorname{Rips}_r(S)\).
† Qualification. Many libraries instead call the pairwise-distance threshold itself the Rips parameter. Always check whether a reported value is a radius \(r\) or a diameter threshold \(2r\).
