From continuous shape to finite data
The coffee cup and doughnut are ideal continuous spaces. A point cloud is a finite set of observations. Even if sampled around a circle, the points do not themselves contain connecting arcs or a loop.
To study topological organisation, we construct a representation that records which observations are treated as related. Its topology belongs first to that representation. A claim about the observed data or underlying system requires an argument connecting the objects.

† Qualification. A visible gap in a scatter plot is not automatically a topological hole. The representation and neighbourhood rule determine whether a homology class is present.


